---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import JsonLd from '~/components/common/JsonLd.astro';
import { SITE } from 'astrowind:config';

const metadata = {
  title: `SUP Fin Systems Guide — US Fin Box, Slide-In, and Thruster Setup | ${SITE.name}`,
  description:
    'Comprehensive guide to SUP fin systems: US fin box, slide-in, click-in, and thruster setups. How fin shape, size, and placement affect tracking, stability, and maneuverability.',
  ignoreTitleTemplate: true,
};
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'SUP Fin Systems Guide',
  description: metadata.description,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name },
  datePublished: '2026-07-27',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <JsonLd data={jsonLd} />
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-8">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-sky-600">{SITE.name}</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><a
        href="/research"
        class="hover:text-sky-600">Research</a
      ><span class="mx-2">/</span><span class="text-gray-900">SUP Fin Systems</span>
    </nav>
    <div class="mb-4">
      <span
        class="inline-block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-600 bg-sky-50 px-3 py-1 rounded-full"
        >Engineering</span
      ><span class="ml-3 text-sm text-gray-400">7 min read</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">SUP Fin Systems Guide</h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-gray-500 mb-12 leading-relaxed">
      The fin system is the underwater control surface that determines how your SUP tracks, turns, and holds in various
      water conditions. Fin choice can dramatically change board behavior.
    </p>
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:text-gray-900 prose-p:text-gray-600">
      <h2>US Fin Box</h2>
      <p>
        The most common fin attachment system on higher-quality SUPs. A molded plastic box is welded into the board hull
        with a threaded insert. The fin slides into the box and is secured with a set screw (usually M6 or M8 thread).
        The US fin box accepts standard US fin shapes from 4.5" to 9" long. This quick-change system lets paddlers swap
        fins without tools beyond an Allen key. US fin boxes are preferred on touring and performance boards where fin
        tuning matters.
      </p>
      <h2>Slide-In Fin System</h2>
      <p>
        A simpler system where the fin has a molded tab that slides into a corresponding slot on the board. The fin is
        held by friction or a small click-lock mechanism. Slide-in fins are common on entry-level and mid-range SUPs
        because they are cheaper to manufacture than US fin boxes. The main limitation is a fixed fin position — you
        cannot adjust fin placement fore/aft for different handling characteristics. Slide-in fins are adequate for
        recreational paddling but limit performance tuning.
      </p>
      <h2>Thruster (3-Fin) Setup</h2>
      <p>
        Three-fin configurations use a large center fin flanked by two smaller side fins. The side fins provide
        additional lateral resistance for improved tracking in crosswinds and chop. On inflatable SUPs, thruster setups
        typically use a US fin box for the center fin and molded side fin boxes. This configuration is preferred on
        touring boards where maintaining course in variable conditions matters.
      </p>
      <h2>Fin Shape and Performance</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Long narrow fin (7-9"):</strong> Maximum tracking, best for touring and flatwater. More drag, harder to
          turn.
        </li><li>
          <strong>Short wide fin (4.5-6"):</strong> Quick turning, less tracking. Best for surfing and river running.
        </li><li>
          <strong>Balanced dolphin fin (6"):</strong> Compromise between tracking and turning. Good all-around choice for
          most conditions.
        </li><li>
          <strong>Touring fin (8-9"):</strong> Deep with swept-back shape for efficient tracking with minimal weed catching.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Fin Placement</h2>
      <p>
        Fin position (distance from the tail) affects turning radius and stability. Fins positioned farther forward
        create a tighter turning radius but reduce straight-line stability. Fins positioned farther back increase
        tracking stability but require more force to turn. US fin boxes with adjustable screw positions allow tuning fin
        placement across a range of 2-3 inches.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
